Subject: [PATCH 04/12] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 5, patch 4;
 built on 5c7abd72a with the ten patches of series 4, the move of the old
 front pages and the first three of series 5 applied): THE GUARDS OF WHAT IS
 SEALED READ EVERY COMMIT (Review 79, the second reader's row 23). Three
 guards hold that a sealed file, a list of hashed files under its usual name,
 and a list under any other name, does not change after it is added. Each read
 main's own line only. A branch that adds such a file, changes it, and is then
 merged shows the file on main's line as added, and the change was read by
 nothing. And the third guard read the file as it stood before a change as
 text: a list whose bytes are not text raised an error in place of an answer.
 THE MEND: the three guards share one reader of every commit that the head
 reaches and their root does not. A commit with one parent is read beside it,
 all such commits in one call, as before. A MERGE IS READ FOR WHAT IT CHANGES
 ITSELF: a path is the merge's own change only where its state in the merge is
 the state in none of its parents. What a merge brings from a branch was
 changed on that branch and is read there, so a change that is waived by name
 is not counted again at the merge; and what a branch from before a seal lacks
 is no deletion. The file as it stood is read as bytes. The messages are word
 for word what they were, and on main the three sentences are the same: no
 commit after any of the three roots stands on another line today. What it
 does not reach: a file that is added on a branch already changed, in one
 commit, is added; the guards hold what stood in a commit. The lock: three
 tests added to tests/test_gates_are_a_function_of_the_commit.py. On the
 commit before, two fail: a sealed file and two lists changed on a branch
 before the merge are read by nothing, and a list whose bytes are not text
 raises an error. The third holds on both sides, and is the control of the
 reading of merges: a merge that rewrites a list in its own commit is refused,
 and a merge that brings in a branch from before the seals is not. Tested
 against planted errors; computed once. 0 of 19.

